OK, now I have finished checking and updating for 3.4.2 all the patches I
used for 3.3.1. 
Except, of course, for the bugfixes that were specific for 3.4.1.
And the travelcache patch seems to have found its way to the official
3.4.2, so there was no need for revising it again.

There's still one further fix for the dump patch security thing that Viljo
brought up: 
In the dump_init function in end.c, I had code like this:

       strncpy(q, plname, strlen(plname));
       regularize(q);
       q[strlen(plname)] = '\0';
       q += strlen(q);

It should really be like this:

       strncpy(q, plname, strlen(plname)+1);
       regularize(q);
       q += strlen(q);

because the strncpy didn't append the terminating null in the first
version, so the regularize function may have messed up something beyond
the space reserved.
I updated the patch on Monday, but didn't announce it here yet.

Also, Marius Wojsyk found a problem with the sortloot patch. His compiler
complained about "illegal use of this type as an expression" for the winid
tmpwin declaration in the do_containerconts function in end.c, below one
#ifdef SORTLOOT block. Some compilers are picky in requiring variable
declarations in the beginning of the block.. I didn't notice it myself
because gcc didn't complain about it.

But I moved the declaration up, so that it is immediately after the line
                } else if (box->cobj) {
before the #ifdef SORTLOOT line.
This has no effect on the functionality of the patch, but enables
compilation with compilers that gave compilation errors before the change.

The color Hp monitor patch, Pasi's menucolor patch, showscore_hide,
deathexplore patch and German keyboard patch worked as they were, the
patch utility just gave some offset warnings as expected and some fuzz
warnings, but no rejects IIRC. Pasi updated the menucolor diff and I made
new diffs for the other patches.

The forgetquit patch also worked as it did, but while I was at it, I made
a little change: I changed the FORGET_EXPLORE condition to PROMPT_LOGEXP
and with that defined, it now prompts for making a logfile entry of wizard
or explore mode games. (The earlier version always skipped it with
FORGET_EXPLORE, and without the patch the game always writes them to the
logfile.) Earlier I had the condition in topten.c but now I moved it to
end.c because all the window structures are deleted before the topten
call, so the prompt can't be called from there. As a side effect the
topten list snippet is now not displayed when you decide to skip the
logwriting. 

And yes, I also wrapped up a combined diff of all of the patches I have
compiled in my copy of the game. As before, you can find it at

http://www.netsonic.fi/~walker/nethack.html

I guess now I'll have some more time for actually playing the game again..
unless somebody finds more bugs in those patches.

